# Copy to the repo root as `.env` (done automatically by `make up`, or by
# `make init`, which also generates a fresh secret key) and fill in secrets.
# NEVER commit the real .env - it is git-ignored. Every variable the stack
# reads is documented here.
#
# How this template and a local .env stay in sync:
#   - `make env-check` (auto-runs before up/rebuild/warm) warns when .env lacks
#     a key this template defines; `make sync-env` appends the missing ones
#     with the defaults below. Both read only UNCOMMENTED lines.
#   - Commented keys are deliberate: their code defaults ARE the intended
#     values. A key is declared live only when leaving it unset is itself a
#     problem (as SECRET_MASTER_KEY is).
#   - When a change renames a key or changes a default's VALUE, a MIGRATION
#     NOTE is added here - sync-env only appends missing keys, it cannot fix a
#     stale value. Notes are pruned once deployments have caught up; the full
#     history lives in git.
#
# MIGRATION NOTE (2026-08-08) - MAX_STREAMS_PER_TENANT counts something new.
# It caps concurrent chat streams PER USER now, where it used to cap them per
# workspace. The key and its default are unchanged, so env-check and sync-env
# see nothing to report - but the ceiling on how much generation a whole
# deployment can run at once is now the value TIMES the number of people
# signed in, not the value. A single-user deployment is unaffected. Anyone who
# raised this to protect a shared GPU should re-read it as a per-person budget
# and lower it accordingly.
#
# MIGRATION NOTE (2026-08-09) - DEFAULT_CONTEXT_WINDOW is new, and the model
# profile no longer decides the window for an unsized tag. `make sync-env`
# appends the key with its 8192 default, which is what the dev profile served
# before; a deployment on a larger profile is seeded to the number it was
# already running (16384 on mid / server-t4, 32768 on server-a10) by writing
# that value into its WORKSPACE context ceiling, so nothing changes shape on
# upgrade. Three consequences worth knowing. The workspace ceiling now REPLACES
# this default for an unsized tag rather than only clamping it, so raising the
# ceiling on the console genuinely raises the window (still never past the
# model's own native limit). And clearing that ceiling on a large-profile
# deployment drops such tags back to DEFAULT_CONTEXT_WINDOW - raise the key
# here first if that is not wanted. A brand-new deployment has no row to seed,
# so it starts at this key's value - set it to the hardware's number when first
# configuring a machine larger than a laptop.
#
# One case the seed cannot get right: a deployment that EDITED the
# `defaults: { num_ctx: ... }` of its own copy of the old model-profile file.
# The seeded numbers are what the shipped profiles declared, so a customised
# one is not restored - re-enter it as the workspace context ceiling, or as
# this key, after upgrading.
#
# MIGRATION NOTE (2026-08-09) - two serving decisions moved out of the model
# profiles, and one of them changes behaviour on upgrade whether or not this
# file is touched. Read both.
#
#   RERANKER_URL is now the ONLY thing that decides whether retrieval
#   re-scores. The profile's `enabled:` flag and its reranker entry no longer
#   vote. This template used to ship the key ACTIVE while the dev profile
#   disabled reranking, so the shipped default looked wired and did nothing -
#   a deployment upgrading with that combination will START reranking. The key
#   is now commented out by default. UNSET it (or comment it out) on any
#   deployment that is not actually running the sidecar, or every turn will
#   try to reach a service that is not there. `make up` starts the sidecar
#   exactly when this key is set, so the two can no longer drift apart.
#
#   EMBEDDING_DEVICE is new, and it DECIDES where embedding work runs -
#   outright, with nothing else consulted. It defaults to `cpu`, which is what
#   the dev profile pinned, so a laptop is unchanged. A mid or server profile
#   used to leave its embedder on `auto` and now gets `cpu` until this key is
#   set to `auto`, which is the one value change worth making deliberately on
#   a machine with card to spare.
#
# The embedding MODEL itself also moved: it is a workspace default now, read
# from the console rather than from a profile. Nothing needs doing on upgrade
# - with no choice stored, the deployment keeps embedding with the model its
# own stored passages were written by, and falls back to the one embedding
# model the server holds when there are no passages and only one candidate.
#
# MIGRATION NOTE (2026-08-10) - the model tier is gone, and with it the
# committed catalog that decided which models a deployment ran. What a
# deployment runs now comes from three places it can actually edit: a
# conversation's pinned model, the workspace defaults on the console, and the
# keys below. What the platform KNOWS about a model comes from the model
# server's own capability report, a code-shipped seed of family quirks, the
# operator declarations stored against the installed weights, and the measured
# tool/think/embedder checks - none of which a file has to be kept current for.
# Line by line:
#
#   MODEL_TIER is REMOVED. Delete the line; a stray one is inert. What it used
#   to decide is now split in two: which models run is the console's Workspace
#   defaults page with DEFAULT_CHAT_MODEL beneath it, and how big a turn may be
#   is DEFAULT_CONTEXT_WINDOW with the workspace context ceiling above it.
#
#   MODELS_YAML_PATH is REMOVED. There is no catalog file to point at. Delete
#   the line; `make sync-env` reports it as a key the template no longer
#   mentions rather than removing it.
#
#   RERANKER_MODEL is NEW, defaulting to bge-reranker-v2-m3 - the model the
#   bundled reranker sidecar is started with. A rerank call has to name a
#   model, and only the sidecar knows which weights it loaded, so this is
#   deployment configuration beside RERANKER_URL. Change it ONLY alongside the
#   sidecar's own `--model-id`, in the same edit.
#
#   AND THE ONE THING TO ACT ON: a machine whose models were chosen by its
#   tier now has nothing choosing them. Name the chat model on the console's
#   Workspace defaults page (or in DEFAULT_CHAT_MODEL below) and the embedder
#   on that same page, because nothing infers either from the hardware any
#   more. Two rescues cover the common cases and neither is a substitute for
#   naming one: a server holding exactly one chat model has it adopted as the
#   workspace default on the first turn, and a server holding exactly one
#   embedding model is embedded with unless a workspace names another. Several
#   installed embedders and no stored choice means retrieval REFUSES rather
#   than picking, since the wrong pick writes vectors into a space the other
#   model can never read.
#
#   ORDER MATTERS ON ONE UPGRADE, ONCE. The migration that seeds each
#   workspace's context ceiling reads MODEL_TIER from the environment AT
#   UPGRADE TIME, to give a box the window it was already serving. A machine
#   that has not yet run `make db-upgrade` since 2026-08-09 must therefore run
#   migrations BEFORE the tier key leaves its .env, or every workspace is
#   seeded at the 8192 fallback instead of the 16384 or 32768 its hardware was
#   serving - a silent quartering of the window, with nothing to point at. A
#   machine already past that migration is unaffected: the ceiling is stored,
#   and the key is never read again. Either way the ceiling is editable in the
#   console afterwards.
#
# Two make targets went with the tier. `make pull-models` is retired: models
# are downloaded from the Model Manager console page at /console/models, which
# checks the tag against the registry first and runs a fast tool check after.
# The "Getting models onto the machine" section of docs/SETUP.md carries the headless
# equivalent for a box with no browser to hand. And `make init` no longer takes
# PRESET - it still writes this template to .env and generates
# SECRET_MASTER_KEY, with nothing to select.

# ── Deployment layout ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# What KIND of machine this is, which is what decides how the stack is
# assembled. Every make target reads it, so `make up` is the right command on a
# server and on a development checkout alike, and neither can be brought up in
# the other's shape by accident.
#
#   prod  the layout the platform is built to run in: prebuilt images,
#         restart: always, and NOTHING published except the Caddy reverse proxy
#         on 80 and 443, which serves the web UI and the API from one HTTPS
#         origin.
#   dev   the development layout, and a deliberate exception to it: the app
#         hot-reloads from the checkout, and the backing services publish host
#         ports (on loopback) so the database, the vector store and the model
#         server can be inspected while the code is being worked on.
#
# The template ships `dev` because a fresh checkout is usually being read and
# edited rather than served, so `make init && make up` works with nothing filled
# in first. A machine that serves anybody sets `prod` here - it is the first of
# the handful of keys a deployment changes, and docs/DEPLOYMENT.md
# lists the rest in one table.
#
# It is a separate axis from PROFILE (where models are served) - a server whose
# GPU is a remote pod is DEPLOY_ENV=prod together with PROFILE=remote.
#
# To override it for ONE command, write it after the target - `make up
# DEPLOY_ENV=prod`. The env-prefix form (`DEPLOY_ENV=prod make up`) is silently
# ignored while this key exists here, because make reads this file as part of
# its own makefile and a makefile assignment outranks the environment.
DEPLOY_ENV=dev

# --- DEPLOY_ENV=prod only --------------------------------------------------
# Both are read by the Caddy container and by nothing else, so both sit idle in
# the development layout and both matter the moment a machine serves anybody.
#
# The public hostname this deployment answers on. Set it to a name whose DNS
# A/AAAA record already points here, and Caddy requests a publicly-trusted
# certificate on first start and renews it for as long as the stack runs, with
# nothing to install or rotate by hand.
#
# BLANK is the fallback for a machine with no DNS record yet: Caddy then serves
# `localhost` with a certificate from its own internal authority, so HTTPS works
# immediately (browsers will warn that the certificate is untrusted, which is
# accurate). Filling the hostname in later switches the same configuration over
# with no other edit.
DOMAIN=
# Contact address registered with the certificate authority. Set it on any
# deployment answering on a real hostname: it is the only channel through which
# a renewal problem is reported before it becomes an outage. Certificates do
# issue without it, so it can stay blank while DOMAIN is.
ACME_EMAIL=

# ── Product name ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# What this deployment calls itself. One key names the whole thing: the browser
# tab, the sign-in card and the heading above the conversation list all show
# it, and the backend titles its API documentation with it.
#
# Left unset, the working name below is used, so a deployment with no opinion
# about this never has to think about it. Set it and everything answers to the
# new name after a restart - no rebuild is involved, because the frontend reads
# the value when a page is requested rather than compiling it into the bundle.
#
# It is display text only. Nothing is stored under it and nothing keys on it,
# so changing it later is safe; the one visible trace is the title field of a
# regenerated API schema.
# APP_NAME=AI Chatbot Platform

# ── Ports & service addresses ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where everything listens (dev layout; the prod layout publishes nothing but
# Caddy) and the addresses the backend dials other services on. The URLs use
# compose service names, so they are right for the bundled containers; point
# one elsewhere only when that service runs outside this stack. The one
# routinely changed is OLLAMA_URL, which lives in the model-serving section
# below beside the remote-pod settings it works with.
#
# Which host address the published ports attach to. The default keeps every
# backing service (Postgres, Redis, Qdrant, Ollama, MinIO) and the backend on
# loopback, so only the frontend is reachable from another machine.
#
# Worth knowing before widening it: on a Linux host, publishing a port writes a
# rule into Docker's own firewall chain, and that chain is consulted before the
# one a host firewall such as ufw manages - so blocking a published port with
# ufw does not work. Loopback binding is what actually closes it. None of this
# affects traffic between containers, which never uses these ports.
#
# Set 0.0.0.0 to publish on every IPv4 interface (reaching the stack from
# another machine on a trusted network). A public server wants the default,
# with a reverse proxy in front. Naming an address publishes IPv4 only, so host
# tooling is better off dialling 127.0.0.1 than localhost.
BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1
BACKEND_PORT=8000
FRONTEND_PORT=3000
POSTGRES_PORT=5432
REDIS_PORT=6379
QDRANT_HTTP_PORT=6333
QDRANT_GRPC_PORT=6334
# The local Ollama container's published port (the backend dials OLLAMA_URL).
OLLAMA_PORT=11434
# MinIO S3 port + web console (starts only under `--profile minio`).
MINIO_PORT=9000
MINIO_CONSOLE_PORT=9001
# Redis backs the cache, the arq job broker, and the rate-limit counters.
REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379/0
QDRANT_URL=http://qdrant:6333
# Reranker sidecar (Infinity serving bge-reranker-v2-m3): a second, sharper
# pass over the passages dense search returned, so the few that reach the model
# are the few that answer the question. It belongs in the standard retrieval
# path and costs little enough to stay there: the bundled sidecar reserves no
# GPU and runs on the CPU, roughly 1-2 seconds per retrieval at realistic
# candidate counts (measured on a laptop-class CPU; server CPUs match or beat
# it), holding about 1.6GB once loaded and more while it loads.
#
# Setting this IS the switch: a URL here means a sidecar is running and
# retrieval re-scores its candidates through it, and leaving it unset keeps
# retrieval dense-only. Nothing else votes - so set it only where the container
# is actually up. `make up` starts the sidecar (and adds its compose profile)
# exactly when this key is set, so the two cannot drift apart. A workspace can
# still switch reranking off for itself from the console; nothing can switch it
# on without a sidecar to reach.
#
# It ships commented out because this template ships the development layout,
# which is the one shape with a real reason to skip it: a memory-constrained
# development host - an 8GB virtual machine, say - cannot hold the reranker
# beside a warmed chat model and a warmed embedder without exhausting itself.
# A machine with the memory to spare uncomments the line.
# RERANKER_URL=http://reranker:7997
# Which model that sidecar serves, as it was started with (`infinity v2
# --model-id ...` in infra/docker-compose.yml). A rerank request has to carry a
# model name and only the sidecar knows which weights it loaded, so the value
# lives here rather than being looked up. The short name below is one the
# bundled sidecar accepts as-is, so the shipped pair needs no adjustment; serve
# different weights and this moves with the sidecar's `--model-id` in the same
# edit, or every rerank call is rejected.
# RERANKER_MODEL=bge-reranker-v2-m3
# SearXNG web-search sidecar (self-hosted metasearch, no API key; runs by
# default). Blank disables web search entirely; see the Web search section.
SEARXNG_URL=http://searxng:8080
# Origins allowed to call the API cross-origin. Comma-separated list (a JSON
# array like ["http://localhost:3000"] is also accepted).
CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000

# ── Model selection ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHICH MODELS RUN is not set here. It is chosen where a person can see what
# the model server actually holds: the workspace defaults on the console
# (Console > Defaults) pick the chat model and the embedder, a conversation can
# pin a different chat model for itself, and DEFAULT_CHAT_MODEL below is the
# deployment-wide fallback beneath both. Nothing in this file describes a
# hardware class, because nothing needs to: what a machine can run is decided
# by which models are pulled onto it and by DEFAULT_CONTEXT_WINDOW.
#
# Which ModelProvider the backend uses: "ollama" (default) | "fake".
# "fake" is deterministic and needs no weights - handy for a UI/SSE smoke test
# before pulling any models or starting Ollama.
MODEL_PROVIDER=ollama

# System turn prepended to every chat request. Anchors small local models to
# English and a consistent persona - without one they tend to code-switch (often
# into Chinese). Leave empty to disable. A sensible default is baked into the
# backend, so this only needs setting to override it.
# A workspace system prompt saved in the console (Workspace defaults > Answering)
# sits ABOVE this and wins whenever it is set; only the set-but-empty value here
# disables the system prompt entirely, which the console field cannot do.
# SYSTEM_PROMPT=You are a helpful AI assistant. Always respond in clear English.

# ── Model serving (Ollama: chat / embeddings / vision) ──────────────────────
OLLAMA_URL=http://ollama:11434
#
# REMOTE MODEL SERVER. The default above names the Ollama container this compose
# file starts. Serving models from a cloud GPU pod instead takes three settings
# that only work together:
#
#   OLLAMA_URL           the address the pod provider's HTTP proxy publishes,
#                        e.g. https://<the proxied address>. The https matters:
#                        those proxies answer plain http with a redirect, and
#                        the backend does not follow redirects - it reports the
#                        redirect and stops, rather than replaying a request
#                        (and its credentials) at an address nobody chose.
#   PROFILE=remote       "no local Ollama container". Add it as a line here -
#                        make reads this file, so it stands for every target
#                        with nothing extra to type. The stack then starts
#                        without an Ollama service, and the model targets talk
#                        to OLLAMA_URL over HTTP. A command-line
#                        `make up PROFILE=remote` overrides it for a one-off.
#   OLLAMA_AUTH_HEADERS  the credential the proxy checks (below).
#
# How to put an authenticating proxy in front of a pod's Ollama - and why an
# unprotected one is a real problem - is in infra/remote-ollama/README.md.
#
# Uncomment to make the remote mode standing for every make target:
# PROFILE=remote
#
# Extra HTTP headers sent on EVERY backend call to OLLAMA_URL, written as a JSON
# object mapping header name to header value:
#   OLLAMA_AUTH_HEADERS={"Authorization": "Bearer <token>"}
# Empty (the default) sends no extra headers, which is right for the local
# container: Ollama has no authentication of its own, and needs none on a
# private network. It matters for a REMOTE endpoint, where a reverse proxy in
# front is the only thing standing between the GPU and the open internet.
#
# Two things to watch. A literal `$` in the value must be written `$$`, or
# compose reads it as a variable reference and substitutes it away. And the
# value is a SECRET - it belongs in this file only, and nothing echoes it back.
OLLAMA_AUTH_HEADERS=
# Where a model name is checked before it is downloaded (does this tag exist,
# how large is it). Only change it to point at a registry mirror or a proxy.
OLLAMA_REGISTRY_URL=https://registry.ollama.ai
# The deployment's chat model, and the bottom rung of the chain that picks one:
# a conversation's own pin, then the workspace default set on the console, then
# this. Normally left unset, because the console is the better place to choose
# - it lists what the server actually holds. Set it on a machine configured
# from files alone, or to pin an A/B run to one tag.
#
# Unset on a box nobody has configured is a first-run state rather than a
# fault: the first turn against a server holding exactly one chat model adopts
# that model as the workspace default, and a server holding nothing that can
# chat answers with a plain error naming the fix.
#
#   THINKING_CHAT_MODEL  the reasoning partner of the tag above, for a model
#                        that cannot reason on request. The literal value
#                        `none` disables pair-routing outright; empty means
#                        "unset" and defers to whatever was declared for the
#                        tag on the console (compose forwards an unset key as
#                        empty, so empty cannot mean "disabled").
#
# Why a pair exists at all: some tags always reason and cannot be told not to,
# and some never do. The plain `qwen3:4b` tag is the first kind (Qwen's
# 2507-refresh thinking variant) and `qwen3:4b-instruct` the second, so a
# deployment running them pairs one with the other; a single hybrid checkpoint
# whose think flag genuinely toggles needs no pair at all.
# DEFAULT_CHAT_MODEL=
# THINKING_CHAT_MODEL=
# How long Ollama keeps a model resident after use. -1 keeps a warmed model
# loaded indefinitely - the fastest replies, and the right default for a
# deployment whose GPU exists to serve models (`make cool` unloads on demand).
# Set a bounded value (e.g. 15m) on a testing/shared box where the card is
# also used for other work and idle VRAM has a cost. Warm with `make warm`.
OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=-1
# How many models Ollama keeps loaded at once. 2 = the GPU chat model plus the
# embedding model (which EMBEDDING_DEVICE pins to the CPU by default, so it
# takes a runner slot but no VRAM); at 1, every retrieval would evict the
# warmed chat model and pay a multi-second reload.
OLLAMA_MAX_LOADED_MODELS=2
# The two ceilings on a COLD MODEL LOAD - not a cost paid per request. They
# move together, and the backend's must stay the LONGER one: a backend that
# hangs up first makes Ollama ABORT a load the server would have finished
# (that abort was killing every warm on a busy box - a healthy load can run
# past six minutes under host contention, measured live 2026-07-29).
#   OLLAMA_LOAD_TIMEOUT          the Ollama server's own give-up ceiling
#                                (its built-in default is 5m)
#   OLLAMA_READ_TIMEOUT_SECONDS  the backend's read timeout toward Ollama
OLLAMA_LOAD_TIMEOUT=15m
OLLAMA_READ_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=960
# The context window a turn asks for when no workspace has named a context
# ceiling of its own. It is the deployment's answer to "how much of this
# machine may one turn spend", which nothing in the code can know: the window
# that actually runs is the smaller of this and the model's own native window,
# so raising it never asks a model for more than it has.
#
# 8192 is a conservative STARTING value chosen to boot anywhere, not a
# recommendation. A runtime asked for a model's full native window allocates
# KV cache for all of it, and modern tags declare enormous ones (131072 on the
# llama 3 family, 262144 on the qwen3 2507 refresh) - allocations that fit
# almost nowhere. Raising it to what the hardware affords is part of first
# configuring a server rather than later tuning: more window is more history and
# more retrieved context per answer, paid for in VRAM.
#
# A workspace context ceiling, set from the console's workspace defaults,
# REPLACES this number for that workspace - upward as well as downward, still
# bounded by what the model itself declares.
DEFAULT_CONTEXT_WINDOW=8192
# Where embedding work runs: "cpu" (default) | "auto". This is the whole
# decision, because placement is a fact about THIS machine - how much card
# there is, and what else wants it.
#
# "auto" lets the model server place it, which is the answer on a card with room
# to spare: ingesting a large document gets several times faster. The default is
# "cpu" because that is the value that is safe on the smallest card - where a
# GPU fits one model, an embedder sharing it evicts the chat model on every
# retrieval and every upload, and the reload costs far more than the slower
# embedding does.
#
# The embedding MODEL is not set here. It defines the vector space, so it lives
# in the workspace defaults on the console, where changing it is a decision
# somebody makes rather than a line somebody edits.
EMBEDDING_DEVICE=cpu

# ── Postgres ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
POSTGRES_USER=chatbot
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me-in-real-env
POSTGRES_DB=chatbot
# DATABASE_URL is derived from the POSTGRES_* values by compose, so the password
# lives in exactly one place. Set DATABASE_URL explicitly only to override it
# (e.g. to point at an external database).
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://user:password@host:5432/dbname

# ── Web search ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which SearchProvider the backend builds: "searxng" (default) | "none". The
# counterpart to MODEL_PROVIDER above, and the explicit way to say web search
# is off: "none" refuses regardless of SEARXNG_URL, where a blank URL leaves
# the question of whether that was deliberate unanswerable. A chat request
# with web_search "off" is fully local regardless.
# SEARCH_PROVIDER=searxng
# SearXNG requires a non-empty secret key. Set a real random value on any
# machine that serves anybody; the placeholder holds up only on a development
# checkout, where this instance is internal-only (no published port, no
# accounts).
SEARXNG_SECRET=change-me-in-real-env
# Master switch for auto/on web-search chat turns (needs SEARXNG_URL set too).
# WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED=true
# Result snippets fetched and ingested per web-search turn.
# WEB_SEARCH_TOP_K=5
# Read timeout for the SearXNG query, in seconds. Keep it >= SearXNG's own
# outgoing.request_timeout (10s) or the first cold search is cut off mid-fan-out.
# WEB_SEARCH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=12
# Fetch the top result PAGES and ground on their extracted text instead of just
# the search snippet (snippets are often the site's boilerplate description).
# Set false for a snippet-only, lower-latency path on small systems.
# WEB_FETCH_PAGES=true
# Per-page fetch read timeout (seconds); fetches run concurrently.
# WEB_FETCH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=5
# Hard cap on bytes downloaded per page.
# WEB_FETCH_MAX_BYTES=2000000
# Cap on extracted characters kept per page before chunking.
# WEB_FETCH_MAX_CHARS=6000
# Full page text is fetched for only this many TOP results per turn; the rest
# ground on their search snippets (fewer chunks to embed before generation).
# WEB_FETCH_MAX_PAGES=3
# Max pages fetched concurrently per web-search turn.
# WEB_FETCH_CONCURRENCY=5
# A result URL already ingested into this conversation within this window is
# reused as-is: no re-fetch, no re-embed. 0 disables the reuse window.
# WEB_RESULT_CACHE_MINUTES=15

# ── RAG retrieval (defaults are sensible; uncomment to tune) ────────────────
# Master switch for retrieval-augmented chat turns; the /rag ingest and query
# endpoints keep working either way.
# RAG_ENABLED=true
# Chunks reaching the prompt, and dense candidates fetched when reranking.
# RAG_TOP_K=5
# RAG_FETCH_K=20
# Minimum cosine similarity for a retrieved chunk to count as relevant.
# Calibrated for the qwen3-embedding family WITH its query-side instruct
# prefix applied (the wrapper that family's model card prescribes, carried in
# the code-shipped model-quirk seed): related passages score 0.40-0.60,
# unrelated text peaks ~0.31. The floor and the prefix must move together -
# `make embedder-check` measures the bands for another embedder and proposes a
# pair of floors rather than leaving the old ones to lie quietly.
# RAG_MIN_SCORE=0.35
# Relative-relevance gap: drop hits scoring more than this below the top hit,
# so weak topical neighbors are not cited next to a strong match (0 disables).
# RAG_REL_MARGIN=0.10
# Token ceiling for retrieved context per chat turn (1 token ~ 4 chars).
# RAG_TOKEN_BUDGET=1200
# Chunking targets, in approximate tokens.
# RAG_CHUNK_TOKENS=350
# RAG_CHUNK_OVERLAP_TOKENS=50
# Texts per embedding call during ingest.
# RAG_EMBED_BATCH=32
# Lifetime of session-scoped (ephemeral) vectors; the background worker
# sweeps expired ones. Sweep cadences that divide 60 run exactly as written.
# RAG_SESSION_TTL_MINUTES=120
# RAG_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MINUTES=10
# Pause between ingest embedding batches while a chat stream is active.
# RAG_INGEST_PAUSE_SECONDS=0.5

# ── Object storage ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where uploaded attachment bytes live. Two adapters sit behind one seam, and
# STORAGE_BACKEND picks which is built. A typo fails at boot rather than
# quietly writing somewhere unexpected.
#
#   local - a directory inside the container, backed by a Docker volume. The
#           standard for a deployment running on one server.
#   s3    - any S3-compatible object store, and the standard path onward.
#
# `local` is a settled answer rather than a placeholder: on one server it is one
# filesystem holding one copy, the retention windows further down bound how far
# it grows, and `make backup` archives it natively beside the database and the
# vectors. Running an object store on that same disk beside the app would add an
# S3 API without adding any durability - the same disk either way.
#
# `s3` is what to move to when the files should live somewhere the application
# server is not, or when the backend runs as more than one replica. The second
# is not a preference: the replica that answers a download is rarely the one
# that took the upload, so half the files look missing. The move itself is
# undramatic - point the keys below at a real object store, copy the existing
# files across, restart.
STORAGE_BACKEND=local

# --- STORAGE_BACKEND=local -------------------------------------------------
# A path INSIDE the container, backed by the named `attachment_data` volume, so
# uploads survive the container recreate that `make rebuild` performs. Change it
# only alongside that mount. Ignored when STORAGE_BACKEND=s3.
STORAGE_LOCAL_DIR=/data/storage

# --- STORAGE_BACKEND=s3 ----------------------------------------------------
# Point these at a REAL object store: a cloud provider's S3 or S3-compatible
# service, or a MinIO deployment of its own on its own host and disks. That is
# what this mode buys - durability and capacity the application server does not
# have to own. The bucket is created on the first upload if it does not exist.
# None of the five is read while STORAGE_BACKEND=local, so they can be filled in
# and checked before the switch is made.
#
# One honest consequence: `make backup` deliberately does NOT archive
# attachments under s3, and records in its manifest that it did not. A real
# object store carries its own durability, and an archive of the now-empty
# volume would look like a copy of the files while holding none of them.
#
# The bundled MinIO service is a different thing, with two jobs. It is how the
# s3 path gets exercised locally - the same adapter and the same code path,
# against an endpoint on this machine - and it is the building block for a
# dedicated store later. Beside the app on one disk it adds the API without the
# durability, which is why it is not what a single server settles on. Its
# endpoint is the compose service and its credentials are the MINIO_ROOT_* pair
# below - the same secret in two places, so change both together:
#   S3_ENDPOINT_URL=http://minio:9000
#   S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=minioadmin
#   S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=minioadmin
# It sits on its own compose profile and nothing depends on it, so it does NOT
# start under a plain `make up`: run the stack with both profiles, e.g.
# `docker compose --profile gpu --profile minio up`, or the backend will point
# at a service that is not there.
#
# On AWS, leave S3_ENDPOINT_URL unset (the region picks the endpoint) and
# either set an IAM access-key pair here or leave both blank to use the
# instance role the container already runs under - the preferred option, since
# it stores no long-lived secret. Against any OTHER endpoint the key pair is
# required, and the backend refuses to start without it rather than failing on
# the first upload.
#
# Permissions the credential needs on the bucket: GetObject, PutObject and
# DeleteObject for uploads and cleanup, plus ListBucket, which the hourly
# orphan-blob sweep walks the store with. CreateBucket is needed only if the
# bucket does not already exist. A credential that may not inspect the bucket
# still works - the adapter carries on rather than failing every upload - but
# without ListBucket the sweep cannot reclaim anything.
# S3_ENDPOINT_URL=
# S3_BUCKET=attachments
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# S3_REGION=us-east-1

# The bundled MinIO service's credentials. This pair is both the console login
# AND the S3 credential the backend signs with, so setting STORAGE_BACKEND=s3
# against it means copying these two values into S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID /
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY above.
MINIO_ROOT_USER=minioadmin
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=minioadmin

# ── Attachments & vision (defaults are sensible; uncomment to tune) ─────────
# Largest single upload the /attachments endpoint accepts, in bytes. A larger
# file is refused with 413 before it is buffered or parsed. Bare pass-through:
# unset keeps the backend default (25000000, i.e. 25 MB).
# ATTACHMENT_MAX_BYTES=25000000
# How long a chat turn waits for a file it names to finish parsing. An upload
# returns as soon as its bytes are stored, so asking about a file right after
# attaching it is the normal case: without this wait the answer would quietly
# ignore it. Past the cap the turn answers anyway and says the file was not used.
# ATTACHMENT_WAIT_SECONDS=30
# ATTACHMENT_POLL_SECONDS=0.5
# How many passages a turn draws from EACH file it explicitly names. Separate
# from RAG_TOP_K: a named file is not competing with the knowledge base for a
# slot, it is the thing being asked about.
# RAG_ATTACHMENT_TOP_K=12
# Token ceiling for the files a turn names, shared between them. Far larger than
# RAG_TOKEN_BUDGET on purpose: that cap stops a knowledge base flooding the
# prompt with material nobody asked for, while a file someone attached and asked
# about should arrive whole if the window has room. Both shrink to what the
# model context actually has free, so neither can overflow it.
# RAG_ATTACHMENT_TOKEN_BUDGET=4000
# How many pictures one turn may show a vision-capable model, and how large each
# one is resized to be. Small on purpose, and NOT by analogy with hosted
# platforms - they allow ten or twenty because they carry context windows of
# 128k and up. The binding fact was measured on this stack: an image costs a
# FLOOR of roughly 1030 prompt tokens however small it is, so on an
# 8192-token window every picture is an irreducible eighth of the prompt.
# VISION_MAX_IMAGES=2
# The grid an image is resized to fit, counted in 32x32-pixel cells (one cell is
# one visual token). 1024 is the runtime's own floor: below it the token cost
# stops falling while the detail keeps going, so this is the largest image still
# charged the minimum. Measured, normalizing to it is free up to about 1440p and
# costs only the finest print above. Raising it buys detail at a proportional
# token cost.
# VISION_IMAGE_BUDGET_CELLS=1024
# Bytes one turn will read out of storage for images, across all of them. A
# bound on memory rather than on quality - the cell budget already decides what
# the model sees - so that a turn of several large uploads is not decoded at
# once just to be shrunk.
# VISION_MAX_SOURCE_BYTES=24000000
# How often the worker reclaims attachment garbage: the files of deleted
# conversations, and blobs no database row points at. Deleting a conversation
# already queues its files for removal, so this only bounds how long the
# leftovers of a failure survive. Cadences that divide 60 run exactly as written.
# ATTACHMENT_GC_INTERVAL_MINUTES=60
# How long a blob no row references must sit untouched before that sweep deletes
# it. An upload writes its bytes moments before committing its row, so the grace
# window is what makes it impossible to collect an upload still in flight.
# ATTACHMENT_ORPHAN_GRACE_MINUTES=60
# How long an upload may sit unprocessed before the worker declares it dead and
# marks it failed. Only the worker ever moves a file off "processing", so if the
# worker is killed mid-parse the row would otherwise claim to be in progress
# forever - and whoever uploaded the file waits on a spinner just as long. The
# bound is generous because a single parse attempt may legitimately run for the
# 15-minute job timeout. Re-uploading a file that failed this way starts a
# genuine fresh attempt.
# ATTACHMENT_STALL_MINUTES=30

# --- Attachment retention --------------------------------------------------
# How long a stored file is kept before the worker empties it. Two tiers, with
# deliberately opposite defaults, because the two kinds of file are not the same
# kind of thing.
#
# Expiring a file removes its bytes and its indexed text and KEEPS the database
# row, marked `expired`. That is the point: a transcript that refers to a
# document can go on saying the document was there and has aged out, instead of
# quietly losing the record. An expired file stops being retrievable, stops
# being citable, and is never handed back to a later upload of the same bytes -
# re-uploading it after it expires stores it fresh.
#
# GENERATED_FILE_RETENTION_DAYS covers files the assistant's own tools produced
# (a rendered spreadsheet, an exported document). Nobody chose to keep those -
# a tool makes one as a side effect of answering, and the next question makes
# another - so they are the tier that expires by default. 0 keeps them forever.
# GENERATED_FILE_RETENTION_DAYS=30
#
# ATTACHMENT_RETENTION_DAYS covers files a PERSON sent: uploads and pasted
# blocks. Unset (the default) means no policy at all - they live as long as the
# conversation they belong to and are reclaimed with it. Somebody chose to send
# these, so nothing ages them out until a deployment with a real retention
# policy names a number of days here. 0 and blank both read as unset.
# ATTACHMENT_RETENTION_DAYS=
#
# Both are read by the WORKER: the sweep rides the hourly attachment GC cron,
# so a file is emptied within an hour of crossing its window - late, never
# early.

# OCR reads the text off a SCANNED PDF page (a picture of text, no text layer).
# On by default; it uses the Tesseract binary the image bundles, and degrades to
# text-layer-only where that binary is absent (a scanned page just yields nothing,
# no error). Set OCR_ENABLED=false to skip it entirely. OCR_LANGUAGE is the
# Tesseract language(s), e.g. "eng" or "eng+deu" (the language data must be
# installed in the image). The rest tune it: DPI to render a page at, the
# text-layer character floor below which a page counts as a scan, the per-document
# page cap (OCR is seconds per page), and the larger parse timeout OCR needs.
# OCR_ENABLED=true
# OCR_LANGUAGE=eng
# OCR_DPI=200
# OCR_MIN_CHARS=16
# OCR_MAX_PAGES=20
# OCR_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=300

# ── Chat persistence & streaming (defaults are sensible; override to tune) ───
# Server-side history rebuild budget per request: newest messages are kept up
# to this many approximate tokens (1 token ~ 4 chars) and this many messages.
# HISTORY_TOKEN_BUDGET=4000
# HISTORY_MAX_MESSAGES=20
# Idle seconds between SSE keep-alive comments; 0 disables heartbeats.
# SSE_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS=15
# Concurrent chat streams allowed to EACH USER of a workspace before requests
# get 429 (the GPU serializes generations anyway, so a small cap fails fast
# instead of queueing). The budget is per person, not per workspace: a shared
# one let a single member's long tool turns lock colleagues out. The key keeps
# its name so no existing .env needs editing; a single-user deployment sees no
# change at all.
# MAX_STREAMS_PER_TENANT=2
# Root level for the structured (JSON) logs: DEBUG | INFO | WARNING | ERROR.
# LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# ── MCP (chassis sidecar; Phase 5) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The MCP sidecar wraps an external API-backed datastore (a JS/HTTP API that
# fronts a database). Upstream secrets are injected into the sidecar at request
# time, never persisted.
#
# One chassis container mounts several wrapper servers, each under its own path
# prefix - so the URL carries that path. The bundled example wrapper is mounted
# at /example and therefore answers at /example/mcp; the bare /mcp path serves
# nothing. Each path is its own registration (own credential, own tool toggles).
# Leaving this unset offers no MCP tools, which is a normal deployment rather
# than a broken one. It is the bootstrap seed for the server registry: on a
# tenant's first use it is upserted into the mcp_servers table ONCE, then the
# database row's settings are authoritative - changing this later does NOT move
# an existing seeded row (edit it on the Integrations page instead). The row's
# EXISTENCE stays config-owned, though: while this is set, deleting the row is
# refused (a delete would only last until the next boot re-seeded it) - disable
# it on the Integrations page, or unset this to make it deletable.
MCP_CHASSIS_URL=http://mcp-chassis:8765/example/mcp
# Shared workspace credential for the chassis, sent as "Authorization: Bearer
# <key>". It belongs on BOTH sides: the same value reaches the sidecar (which
# then demands it on every MCP request) and the backend (which sends it). Left
# unset on both, the chassis accepts unauthenticated requests - acceptable
# because it publishes no ports and is reachable only from inside the compose
# network. SECRET: keep real values in .env only, never in this template.
# MCP_CHASSIS_API_KEY=
# Per-user credentials (Phase 5 slice 6b). Set MCP_PER_USER_CREDENTIALS=true to
# inject the ACTING USER's own stored credential (SecretStore, USER scope) into
# each tool CALL instead of the shared key above. OFF by default, so the
# shared-key behaviour is unchanged until a deployment opts in (and needs
# SECRET_MASTER_KEY set to store any). With it on, a seeded
# `credential_scope=user` server FAILS CLOSED (an honest tool error) for a user
# who has connected no credential, rather than borrowing the shared key.
# MCP_CHASSIS_CREDENTIAL_NAME is the name a user's chassis credential is
# stored under. Listing always uses the shared key, so its cache never mixes
# callers.
# MCP_PER_USER_CREDENTIALS=false
# MCP_CHASSIS_CREDENTIAL_NAME=mcp-chassis
# Host header allow list for the chassis (comma-separated) - the MCP SDK's
# DNS-rebinding defense, which rejects any request whose Host is not listed with
# a 421. Unset uses the default, covering localhost plus the compose service
# name the backend dials:
#   localhost,localhost:*,127.0.0.1,127.0.0.1:*,mcp-chassis,mcp-chassis:*
# A custom hostname (a renamed service, a proxy in front) must be added here or
# every MCP call 421s. Setting it EMPTY would be the SDK's reject-everything
# state, so the chassis refuses to honour an empty value and falls back to the
# default with a warning rather than 421-ing every request silently.
# MCP_CHASSIS_ALLOWED_HOSTS=
# Backend-side MCP budgets (seconds). A tool CALL is work the user asked for and
# can be waited on; a tool LISTING is discovery that runs before the first token
# of every agentic turn, so it gets a far tighter deadline - a hanging sidecar
# then degrades to "no tools this turn" instead of stalling the turn.
# MCP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30
# MCP_LIST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=5
# How long a listing is reused: a good one for MCP_LIST_CACHE_SECONDS (tool sets
# change on deploys, not per turn), an EMPTY one for the shorter error window -
# so a sidecar that comes back is picked up quickly, but a dead one is not
# re-dialled on every turn.
# MCP_LIST_CACHE_SECONDS=300
# MCP_LIST_CACHE_ERROR_SECONDS=30
# OAuth consent for MCP servers (Phase 5 slice 9; workspace-scoped - the admin
# clicks Connect and approves on the provider's page instead of pasting a key).
# The origin the browser returns to after consent. On a deployment it is the
# PUBLIC origin - https:// plus the DOMAIN above - and it has to be set: left at
# the default below, a provider sends the browser back to a localhost address
# that is not this machine and the connection never completes. That default is
# the development frontend origin, whose /api proxy forwards the callback to the
# backend so one origin serves the whole round trip; the reverse proxy keeps
# exactly that shape in a deployment.
#
# The redirect URI registered with a provider is this value plus
# /api/v1/integrations/oauth/callback. Requires SECRET_MASTER_KEY - the flow
# seals what it stores and answers 503 without one.
# OAUTH_REDIRECT_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000
# How long a started consent stays completable before its state expires.
# OAUTH_AUTHORIZATION_TTL_SECONDS=600
# EXAMPLE_API_BASE_URL=
# EXAMPLE_API_KEY=

# ── Document generation (doc-gen sidecar) ───────────────────────────────────
# A second MCP sidecar, registered exactly like the chassis above. It turns a
# structured spec into a real file - a spreadsheet (xlsx), a document (docx), a
# PDF, a CSV, Markdown, or a chart image (png/svg) - by filling pinned library
# code, never by running anything the model wrote. Its server is mounted at
# /docgen, so it answers at /docgen/mcp. Leaving the URL unset offers no
# document tools; like the chassis URL, it is the bootstrap seed for the server
# registry: the row's settings are authoritative once it exists, and while this
# stays set the row cannot be deleted - disable it on the Integrations page, or
# unset this to release it.
DOCGEN_MCP_URL=http://doc-gen:8765/docgen/mcp
# Shared workspace credential for the sidecar, sent as "Authorization: Bearer
# <key>". Same both-sides rule as MCP_CHASSIS_API_KEY: the value reaches the
# sidecar (which then demands it) and the backend (which sends it). Unset on
# both, the sidecar accepts unauthenticated requests - acceptable because it
# publishes no ports and is reachable only from inside the compose network.
# SECRET: keep real values in .env only, never in this template.
# DOCGEN_API_KEY=
# The name the seeded registry row - and so its stored credential - is filed
# under. Its credential scope is always WORKSPACE: the sidecar reaches no
# upstream system, so there is no per-user account to connect.
# DOCGEN_CREDENTIAL_NAME=doc-gen
# Host header allow list for the sidecar (comma-separated) - the same
# DNS-rebinding defense as the chassis, defaulting to:
#   localhost,localhost:*,127.0.0.1,127.0.0.1:*,doc-gen,doc-gen:*
# A custom hostname must be added here or every call 421s; an EMPTY value falls
# back to the default with a warning rather than rejecting everything.
# DOCGEN_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# ── Tool calling / agency ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How much the model drives a turn: agentic (the model decides when to search
# the web or the stored records) | assisted (today's automatic pre-fetch, plus
# tools offered on top - a model that never calls one behaves exactly as today)
# | deterministic (no tools, the pre-tool pipeline verbatim) | auto (defer to
# the ceiling declared for the model itself). Clamps DOWN from that ceiling,
# never up.
AGENCY_MODE=agentic
# Note that "agentic" is EARNED in every deployment: a model reaches it only
# with a DEEP tool-check verdict recorded for the exact installed weights
# (make tool-check; digest-bound, and a re-pull that changes the digest
# auto-reverts the model to unverified). A declaration alone carries a model to
# "assisted" - tools offered, the automatic pre-fetch and knowledge base still
# running - which is what lets a fresh clone answer grounded turns on day one.
# Hardened deployments: when true, even that floor goes, so a model may drive
# only on what the measurement log proves for these weights and anything never
# probed runs deterministic. Default false.
# AGENCY_REQUIRE_VERIFICATION=false
# Loop bounds for one agentic turn: model<->tool round trips, then total calls.
# TOOL_MAX_ITERATIONS=4
# TOOL_MAX_CALLS_PER_TURN=8
# Wall-clock budget for a single tool call (seconds). >= a healthy web_search,
# which now ingests and cites its results: SearXNG's 12s query + the page
# fetches + chunk embedding (a cold CPU embed load alone is 6-8s) + read-back
# retrieval. Measured live: the first post-boot web call needs more than 45.
# TOOL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=75
# Tool-result window budget (approx tokens): per-result cap, turn-wide ceiling,
# and the floor below which tools are NOT offered at all (no room, no tools).
# TOOL_RESULT_MAX_TOKENS=1000
# TOOL_RESULT_TOTAL_MAX_TOKENS=2000
# TOOL_RESULT_MIN_TOKENS=256
# How long a suspended side-effecting-tool confirmation stays resumable (seconds).
# A turn that pauses for the user's OK checkpoints itself; past this the resume is
# refused and the turn is started over. Long enough to decide, short enough that a
# forgotten prompt does not linger for hours.
# TOOL_CONFIRMATION_TTL_SECONDS=900
# Tool selection, for deployments that register enough MCP servers to crowd the
# model's tool list. At or below TOOL_SELECTION_THRESHOLD candidates, selection
# does not run at all: the whole set is offered and the turn pays nothing. Above
# it, the TOOL_SELECTION_TOP_K tools whose descriptions best match the turn are
# offered, plus web_search and search_knowledge, which are always offered. The
# gate is deliberate: a plain agentic turn pays no embedding today, and the dev
# embedder is CPU-pinned (a cold load alone is 6-8s), so ranking must stay inert
# until the tool count is a real problem. The shipped default discovers 7 tools
# (2 local, 3 from the chassis example wrapper, 2 from the doc-gen sidecar); the
# one side-effecting tool is withheld from a client that cannot confirm it and
# offered (behind the confirmation flow) to one that can - so 6 or 7 are offered,
# both still under 8, so selection never runs out of the box.
# TOOL_SELECTION_THRESHOLD=8
# TOOL_SELECTION_TOP_K=5
# Files a tool HANDS BACK (a generated document). Each one is stored as an
# attachment on the conversation and announced to the browser; the model itself
# only ever reads the tool's one-line summary, never the bytes. The first cap is
# per FILE and is deliberately below ATTACHMENT_MAX_BYTES - a file nobody chose
# to upload should not be able to occupy more of the store than one somebody
# did. The second is per TURN, shared across every tool call in it. Anything
# over either cap is dropped with a plain notice in the answer and on the tool
# card, never silently. The per-file value sits just under what an MCP response
# can carry (a 2 MB body, and base64 costs 4 bytes per 3), so THIS is the cap
# that fires and drops one file - above it the transport refuses the body and
# the whole tool call fails instead. Raising it means raising the transport cap
# in the same breath; see app/providers/mcp/source.py.
# TOOL_ARTIFACT_MAX_BYTES=1400000
# TOOL_ARTIFACT_MAX_PER_TURN=10

# ── Integration secrets (SecretStore) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Base64 AES key that encrypts stored upstream credentials at rest. BLANK is a
# real state: the integration surface answers 503 and stores nothing - it never
# falls back to plaintext and never mints a key implicitly (a minted key looks
# fine in dev and destroys every stored secret on the next redeploy, when a
# different one is minted). Must decode to exactly 32 bytes (AES-256): a raw
# pass-phrase is rejected, not silently accepted as a shorter key. Generate one
# with:
#   python -c "import os,base64;print(base64.b64encode(os.urandom(32)).decode())"
# On a fresh checkout `make init` fills this in; an existing .env (which init
# refuses to overwrite) needs it set by hand.
# Once credentials are stored, changing this key makes every one of them
# undecryptable - it is not a value to rotate casually.
# The key is declared here BLANK rather than commented out on purpose: a
# commented line is invisible to `make env-check`, so a checkout that predates
# this variable got no warning that it lacked the one setting the Integrations
# page needs, and discovered it as a failed write mid-form.
SECRET_MASTER_KEY=

# ── Request-rate limiting (Phase 8; defaults are generous) ──────────────────
# Abuse protection, not a quota. A chat turn is ONE request that then streams
# for many seconds, so a person cannot come close to the general budget; a
# script hammering the port can. If a real user ever feels these numbers, the
# numbers are wrong.
#
# Limits are counted per minute, per client, in fixed windows - so a caller can
# spend one budget at the end of a minute and another at the start of the next.
# That burst is accepted deliberately: the point is to stop sustained abuse,
# and a sliding window costs more bookkeeping than it buys here.
#
# Set false to switch limiting off entirely (the app then does no counting at
# all). The counter lives in Redis; if Redis is unreachable the limiter lets
# requests through and logs that it is not enforcing - a bookkeeping outage
# must not become a site outage.
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true
# Ordinary /api/v1 traffic.
# RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=240
# The few routes where one call costs real resources: a model download, an
# embedding pass over a document, an upload's bytes, or an outbound call to a
# third party made on the caller's behalf (integration test-connection and the
# OAuth consent flow), plus the two model probes, which evict the resident
# model and reload it.
# RATE_LIMIT_HEAVY_PER_MINUTE=10
# The one route a healthy client POLLS: attachment status. The composer polls
# every parsing file concurrently every 1.2s, so several files in flight is
# hundreds of requests a minute from one entirely legitimate user. It gets its
# own generous ceiling so the general limit above can stay meaningful rather
# than being raised for everything.
# RATE_LIMIT_LIGHT_PER_MINUTE=1200
# Peer addresses whose X-Forwarded-For header may be believed, comma separated.
# Each entry may be a plain address or a CIDR range (e.g. 172.16.0.0/12), since
# the address a container dials from is assigned by the bridge at boot.
#
# EMPTY (the default) means no forwarded header is ever trusted and limits
# count the address the connection actually came from - correct only for a stack
# reached directly, with nothing relaying to it. Trusting the header from just
# anybody would let a caller mint a fresh identity per request and walk straight
# through the limiter, so this must name the reverse proxy and nothing else.
#
# It cuts both ways, and this is the case to settle before deploying: when
# anything relays requests to the backend and this is empty, EVERY request
# appears to come from that relay and the whole deployment shares ONE client's
# budget. That already applies to the bundled frontend, whose server-side /api
# routes forward browser calls over the internal network - which is why a
# deployment sets the Docker bridge range, e.g.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=172.16.0.0/12. Safe to trust because the header is read
# from the RIGHT, skipping entries that are themselves trusted, and the proxy
# appends the real client LAST - anything an attacker puts in an inbound
# header sits further left and is never the entry selected.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=

# ── Login (OpenID Connect) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OIDC_ISSUER IS THE MASTER SWITCH, and a deployment sets it. Set, the whole
# surface turns on together: every API route starts requiring a signed-in
# session, the browser sign-in flow at /api/v1/auth/login takes over, and the
# admin-only screens start genuinely refusing members. Nothing here names a
# particular provider - the issuer is asked for its own metadata, so any
# conformant OpenID Connect provider works. For Google the issuer is
# https://accounts.google.com.
#
# Left unset or blank there is no login at all: every request acts as the seeded
# default user, exactly as the app behaved before this section existed. That is
# the development shape - right for a checkout on one machine, for CI, and for
# any stack reached only from the machine it runs on, and wrong for anything
# else.
#
# It must be an https URL (http is accepted only on loopback, for a provider
# running on the same machine during development). The app refuses to boot
# otherwise, and not on principle: this URL's metadata document is what names
# the provider's signing keys, so fetching it in the clear would let anyone on
# the network path substitute keys of their own and forge accepted sign-ins.
#
# ANY DEPLOYMENT REACHABLE FROM A NETWORK SOMEBODY ELSE IS ON SHOULD SET THIS.
# Without it, whoever can reach the port is the default user - and therefore
# holds that user's stored integration credentials by proxy.
# OIDC_ISSUER=
#
# The client registered with that provider. Both are REQUIRED once the issuer
# is set: the app refuses to boot without them rather than failing at the first
# sign-in with a message from somebody else's server. The secret is a secret -
# it is never logged, never returned by an endpoint, and never stored anywhere
# but this file.
# OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
# OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# Bootstrap admins, comma separated. Also REQUIRED once the issuer is set, and
# for a blunt reason: without one, every admitted person is a member and the
# workspace has nobody who can administer it.
#
# It is a STANDING floor, not a one-off. A listed address is an admin at every
# sign-in, so it cannot be demoted from inside the app - the members screen
# refuses and points back here. Removing an address from this list does not
# demote anybody; it stops re-applying the floor, and the role can then be
# changed in the app.
# OIDC_ADMIN_EMAILS=
#
# Who else may sign in. A verified address is admitted when it is an admin
# above, listed here, or its domain is listed - and REFUSED otherwise. That
# matters most on a public provider: without a list, any account anywhere could
# sign in and start using the workspace. Domains may be written with or without
# the leading @.
# OIDC_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=example.com
# OIDC_ALLOWED_EMAILS=
#
# How long a session lasts without use, in seconds (default 7 days). It SLIDES:
# every request re-arms it, so somebody working is never signed out mid-task,
# while a browser left alone stops being a key. Sessions are held server-side,
# so signing out ends one immediately rather than waiting for it to expire.
# SESSION_TTL_SECONDS=604800
#
# The redirect URI to register with the provider is OAUTH_REDIRECT_BASE_URL
# (above, in the OAuth section) plus /api/v1/auth/callback. That one setting is
# now the deployment's single public-origin knob and serves both flows - the
# integrations consent flow and this login - because a deployment has one public
# origin, not one per feature. In a deployment it must be the PUBLIC origin
# (https:// plus DOMAIN); left at the localhost default, the provider sends the
# browser back to an address that is not this machine and no sign-in completes.
#
# The session cookie is marked Secure whenever that origin is https, so a
# deployment behind the reverse proxy gets a cookie that never travels in the
# clear, and a local http stack gets one the browser will actually send.
#
# MIGRATION NOTE: OIDC_ISSUER / OIDC_CLIENT_ID / OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET were
# previously commented-out placeholders that nothing read. They are live now,
# and three keys joined them: OIDC_ADMIN_EMAILS, OIDC_ALLOWED_DOMAINS and
# OIDC_ALLOWED_EMAILS. An existing .env needs no change to keep working - with
# no issuer set the behaviour is what it always was.
